  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nBrief, weak ridging moves in this evening, which will allow for at \nleast brief clearing over many areas. Areas that see the overlap \nbetween an existing sizable snowpack, clearing skies, and light \nwinds tonight into Sunday morning will see one of the coldest \nmornings of the winter so far. In addition to the cold overnight \ntemperatures, forecast soundings indicate fog will be a concern \nheading into Sunday morning. \n\nAs quick as the weak ridging moves in this evening, it gets ushered \naway by late Sunday morning ahead of a larger trough near the \nPacific NW coast. Several weaker waves eject from this weakening \ntroughing toward the Northern Rockies Sunday into Monday. Given the \noverall weakening nature of the system and associated waves, \nprecipitation that falls near the Continental Divide late Sunday \ninto Monday is looking light. \n\nAnother brief stretch of ridging builds in Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight, but quickly gets ushered eastward as a more progressive zonal \nflow develops thereafter toward Thanksgiving. Tuesday and much of \nWednesday are looking dry and more mild at this point, but as the \nzonal flow develops toward Thanksgiving and Friday, a mix of rain \nand snow will sneak back into the forecast near the Continental \nDivide. Cluster guidance is nearly unanimous in an upper level ridge \nbuilding in next weekend, though it struggles to resolve just how \namplified it will be. Weaker ridges are a bit cooler and continue \nopportunities for rain/snow along the Continental Divide while \nstronger ridges trend drier and warmer. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFog Tonight...\n\nUncertainty in the development of fog tonight continues despite \nguidance suggesting that light upslope flow will develop across the \nplains where skies look to at least briefly clear. Combine this with \nan existing snowpack and saturation (fog) would seem quite \nreasonable. Forecast soundings are not particularly excited about \nthis prospect however (mainly due to lingering cloud cover), which \nleads to lower confidence in impactful fog developing in most areas \nat this time horizon. -AM\n\nLow temperatures through Sunday morning...\n\nBiggest source of uncertainty with respect to low temperatures \ntonight will be whether or not skies clear sufficiently to allow \nfor maximum radiational cooling. Even if skies clear during the \novernight hours the development of fog could hinder temperatures \nfrom cooling efficiently despite light winds and a fresh snowpack \nacross most locations. If skies do clear and fog does not develop \nit is possible that overnight temperatures, most notably over the \nplains of Central and North Central Montana, could fall below zero\nconsidering the ~1030-1035mb surface high building south across \nSouthern Alberta and Saskatchewan over the fresh snowpack.\n\nThe table below lists probabilities for low temperatures to fall to \nor below 10 degrees, 0 degrees, and -10 degrees at select locations \nacross North Central, Central, and Southwest Montana through Sunday \nmorning. It should be noted that while the probability for \ntemperatures of 0 degrees or colder is relatively low or even at 0% \nfor most locations, models (including their respective ensembles) \nstruggle to handle ideal radiational cooling conditions when a fresh \nsnowpack is present. For this reason it would not be out of the \nrealm of possibilities for locations like Great Falls to fall below \nzero despite the NBM5.2 probability of 0%. This is simply do to the \nmodels inability to realize this cooling.\n\n                  Sunday Morning Low Temperature Probabilities\nLOCATION                 <= -10  |    <= 0   |   <= 10\nBrowning                   0%    |     5%    |    75%\nCut Bank                  <5%    |    25%    |    90%\nHavre                      0%    |     0%    |    45%\nGreat Falls                0%    |     0%    |    35%\nLewistown                  0%    |     0%    |    90%\nHelena                     0%    |     0%    |    15%\nBozeman                    0%    |     0%    |    45%\nDillon                     0%    |     0%    |     0%\nEnnis                      0%    |     0%    |     0%\nWest Yellowstone           0%    |     0%    |    10%\n\n- Moldan",
